2026-11-07T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-2442) 'latency is the metric for 64 Hz entity state
 # uuid: 7e911ffb-beb2-5e1d-8eae-761b44bc81ad
 # tags: rfd:title, rfd:flight-level:l1
 RFD:Committed  1 RFD
 Serials:VSekaiFabric

2026-11-07T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-2442) 'a 64 Hz 3D state server is judged by tick-to-send time at a held user count.
 # tags: rfd:feature, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-2442) 'the metric, budget, sweep and pass rule for the entity server on one droplet.
 # tags: rfd:scope, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-2442) 'How many users can one shared vCPU serve at 64 Hz before a tick runs late, and what would show it?
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-2442) 'A feed server that holds 295,000 readers at a p99 of 117 ms spends about seven and a half ticks on its tail. It serves reads, so neither its user count nor its latency says anything about a tick.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-2442) 'The first gate: a load generator holds a fixed user count for 5 minutes and prints server-side tick-to-send p50, p95 and p99, and a planted slow handler pushes p99 over budget and fails the run.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-2442) 'latency is the metric for 64 Hz entity state
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2442) 'The user count is whatever 64 Hz allows. Latency is the metric (operator, 2026-11-07).
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2442) '- The tick is fixed at 64 Hz, 15,625,000 ns.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2442) '- The primary figure is server-side tick-to-send time, from packet received to batched datagram handed to the kernel, at p50, p95 and p99. The proposed budget is p99 under 4 ms, a quarter of a tick.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2442) '- Tick overruns, missed deadlines and steal time from the host's CPU counters are recorded beside it, so a hypervisor stall is attributed and not blamed on the code.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2442) '- The rule is pass or fail, not rank. Raise users in a sweep to the knee where p99 crosses the budget. A run passes with 0% errors over a 5 minute hold.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2442) '- Only bytes derived from the code are stated, and no network figure is measured.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2442) '- A figure stays labelled derived until a measured one replaces it.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2442) '- RFD66606-2443, the server that this metric judges.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2442) '- The droplet here is the benchmark host. Where the ghost servers run is open: Fly is a non-goal (RFD66606-2490, operator, 2026-11-12).
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2442) '- Gate evidence: the feed server, CI run 42417791013, job tick-budget (success). It proves a p99 under 4 ms budget on a 64-tick loopback check. The 5-minute hold at fixed user counts and the sweep to the knee are not run yet, so the gate is unproven.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2442) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2442) 'The only public reference is a read-heavy feed benchmark that reports users held and request rate. Neither says whether a 15.625 ms tick completes in time.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2442) 'Derived figures
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2442) 'All of these are arithmetic on the reference cost of 26 microseconds per operation, not measurements.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2442) '- Operations per second are 128 times the user count: 64 inbound and 64 batched outbound per user.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2442) '- One core at 100% does 38,461 operations per second. At 128 users that is 16,384, about 42.6% busy. At 150 users it is 49.9%, the floor with tail headroom. At 300 users it is 99.8%.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-2442) '- The 26 microseconds include HTTP parsing and SQLite reads, which the tick path drops.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-2442) 'Bytes
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-2442) '- An entity packet is 100 bytes, so inbound is 6,400 bytes per second per user.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-2442) '- Twelve entities in view make 1,200 bytes of data and 1,248 bytes with IP and UDP headers, under the 1,280 byte IPv6 minimum. Sixteen make 1,600 bytes and fragment, so more datagrams split them.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-2442) '- Outbound is users times entities times 6,400. At 128 users and 12 entities that is 9,830,400 bytes per second, about 25.5 TB in 30 days. The droplet's transfer allowance is not verified, so egress may bind before the CPU, and the entities in view are the main lever.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-2442) 'Steal time
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-2442) 'A shared vCPU can lose a few milliseconds to the hypervisor, a large share of a tick. Whether the droplet is one shared vCPU is assumed and not verified.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2442  1 LINE
 Text:Written
